{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792508653,"nonce":"rpk7nHVb2wq8kuyRpqsINrd1SnE=","title":"TypeScript: preserve exported checker interfaces through shared artifacts","message":"Native symbols are module-qualified, but alias/property views, function signatures and generic parameters, readonly metadata, array bounds and returned-parameter provenance still exist only in the frontend's private maps. A TypedProgram or lowered snapshot cannot currently supply that information when checking an importer. The generic snapshot regression compensates by parsing concatenated source and removing library bodies.\n\nPreserve a body-free, interned checker interface in the shared TypedProgram/artifact/snapshot path. Publish explicitly exported functions/types and local export aliases, preserving private layouts/views needed by exported signatures. Consume dependency interfaces for named value/type imports with aliases, using shared TypedImport/CompiledImport linking and deferred snapshot bodies. Keep readonly policy, generic specialization, array provenance/minimum bounds, direct GEPs and static release valid across this boundary. Avoid another linker/cache/snapshot container or cloning executable bodies to type-check callers.\n\nAcceptance: independently check/import source and lowered-snapshot libraries without concatenating source; cover readonly and structural views, generics, array aliases/owned returns, private-symbol and bad-import diagnostics, type-only evaluation behavior, interpreter-first and Cranelift/LLVM/mixed execution, selective decoding and existing conformance. Keep the broad native module issue 0c26889a7cd2c4060eb94ef93fd808c2857089dbf714735ca52b5d071bdb1564 open for filesystem/package resolution, re-exports, namespace/default imports and full live-binding/cycle semantics.","files":null}]}